#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:2
msgid "Using ``rosidl::Buffer`` backends"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:6
msgid "Contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:9
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:11
msgid ""
"``rosidl::Buffer<T>`` is the generated C++ representation of variable-length "
"primitive array fields in ROS 2 messages (``uint8[]``, ``float32[]``, ...). "
"It behaves like a ``std::vector<T>`` by default, but its storage is "
"pluggable so that vendors can back those fields with non-CPU memory (for "
"example, GPU memory) and transport them with as few copies as the RMW and "
"the backend allow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:18
msgid ""
"See :doc:`../Concepts/Intermediate/About-Buffer-Backends` for a conceptual "
"overview."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:21
msgid "This guide covers:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:23
msgid ""
"how to keep existing publishers and subscribers working without changes;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:24
msgid "how to opt a subscription in to a specific set of backends;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:25
msgid ""
"how to use a backend's user-facing API to read and write its native data "
"type;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:27
msgid ""
"how ``tensor_msgs/msg/ExperimentalTensor`` and ``torch_conversions`` use the "
"same backend mechanism for tensor data;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:29
msgid "how to make sure publisher and subscriber are configured compatibly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:32
msgid "Default behavior (no changes required)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:34
msgid ""
"If no backend is explicitly configured, ``rosidl::Buffer<T>`` still behaves "
"like ``std::vector<T>``. Existing code such as"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:38
msgid ""
"auto msg = std::make_unique<sensor_msgs::msg::Image>();\n"
"msg->data.resize(width * height * 3);\n"
"std::memcpy(msg->data.data(), source_ptr, msg->data.size());\n"
"publisher_->publish(std::move(msg));"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:45
msgid ""
"continues to compile and run unchanged because ``rosidl::Buffer<uint8_t>`` "
"implicitly converts to ``std::vector<uint8_t> &`` when its active backend is "
"CPU."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:49
msgid ""
"On the subscription side, if a subscription does not opt in to any non-CPU "
"backend (see below), the RMW delivers the data with the CPU backend just "
"like it did before the feature was introduced, so ``msg->data.size()``, "
"``msg->data[i]``, ``msg->data.data()`` and so on keep working."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:56
msgid "RMW support"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:58
msgid ""
"Buffer backends require support from the RMW implementation to negotiate and "
"serialize descriptors on the wire."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:61
msgid ""
"The first supported RMW integration is ``rmw_fastrtps_cpp``. With other RMW "
"implementations, a subscription that requests a non-CPU backend still "
"functions -- it simply receives CPU-backed data, exactly as if "
"``acceptable_buffer_backends`` had not been set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:66
msgid ""
"The non-CPU backend path currently applies to topic publish/subscribe only. "
"Services and actions do not expose an ``acceptable_buffer_backends`` option "
"and continue to use their normal request, response, feedback, status, and "
"result serialization paths."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:70
msgid "Discovering installed backends"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:72
msgid ""
"Backend plugins are discovered through ``pluginlib`` and live in packages "
"whose ``package.xml`` exports the ``rosidl_buffer_backend`` plugin "
"description file. To see which backends are installed in the current "
"environment:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:77
msgid "$ ros2 pkg list | grep _buffer_backend"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:81
msgid "Each backend package usually installs two things:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:83
msgid ""
"the backend plugin itself (a shared library), registered under a short name "
"such as ``cuda`` in the plugin XML;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:85
msgid ""
"a descriptor-message package (``*_msgs``) containing the backend's "
"descriptor ``.msg`` type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:88
msgid ""
"Both must be installed on every node that participates in the topic -- "
"publishers and subscribers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:92
msgid "Enabling a backend on a subscription"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:94
msgid ""
"Publishers do **not** advertise a list of backends; they simply publish "
"whatever backend their buffer currently uses. Subscriptions, on the other "
"hand, opt in to non-CPU backends via the ``acceptable_buffer_backends`` "
"option on ``rclcpp::SubscriptionOptions`` (or the "
"``acceptable_buffer_backends`` keyword argument in ``rclpy``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:100
msgid ""
"The option is a comma-separated string of backend names. It accepts the "
"following forms:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:107
msgid "Value"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:108
msgid "Meaning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:109
msgid "empty or ``\"cpu\"``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:110
msgid "CPU only. This is the default and preserves backward compatibility."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:112
msgid "``\"any\"``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:113
msgid "Accept any backend that is installed in this process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:114
msgid "``\"cuda,mydev\"``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:115
msgid ""
"Accept only the listed backends, in addition to CPU. Names match the plugin "
"``name`` attribute in the backend's plugin XML."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:118
msgid ""
"CPU is always implicitly acceptable, so no matter what is specified, a "
"subscription can always receive CPU-backed messages (for example, when the "
"publisher's backend cannot serve this particular peer)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:123
msgid "C++ example"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:125
msgid ""
"#include <cuda_runtime.h>\n"
"\n"
"#include \"rclcpp/rclcpp.hpp\"\n"
"#include \"sensor_msgs/msg/image.hpp\"\n"
"#include \"cuda_buffer/cuda_buffer_api.hpp\"\n"
"\n"
"class GpuImageSubscriber : public rclcpp::Node\n"
"{\n"
"public:\n"
"  GpuImageSubscriber()\n"
"  : Node(\"gpu_image_subscriber\")\n"
"  {\n"
"    cudaStreamCreate(&stream_);\n"
"\n"
"    rclcpp::SubscriptionOptions sub_opts;\n"
"    sub_opts.acceptable_buffer_backends = \"cuda\";\n"
"\n"
"    subscription_ = this->create_subscription<sensor_msgs::msg::Image>(\n"
"      \"image\", 10,\n"
"      [this](sensor_msgs::msg::Image::ConstSharedPtr msg) {\n"
"        if (msg->data.get_backend_type() == \"cuda\") {\n"
"          // Zero-copy GPU path: read the device pointer directly.\n"
"          auto rh = cuda_buffer_backend::from_input_buffer(msg->data, "
"stream_);\n"
"          process_on_gpu(rh.get_ptr(), msg->data.size(), stream_);\n"
"        } else {\n"
"          // CPU fallback: msg->data behaves like std::vector<uint8_t>.\n"
"          process_on_cpu(msg->data.data(), msg->data.size());\n"
"        }\n"
"      },\n"
"      sub_opts);\n"
"  }\n"
"\n"
"private:\n"
"  rclcpp::Subscription<sensor_msgs::msg::Image>::SharedPtr subscription_;\n"
"  cudaStream_t stream_{nullptr};\n"
"};"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:164
msgid "Two things to note:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:166
msgid ""
"The subscription only declares which backends are *acceptable*; the actual "
"backend of a received message is whatever the publisher sent, subject to "
"negotiation. Always branch on ``msg->data.get_backend_type()`` before using "
"a backend-specific API."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:171
msgid ""
"Backend APIs (``cuda_buffer_backend::from_input_buffer``, "
"``cuda_buffer_backend::from_output_buffer``, ...) are provided by the "
"backend package, not by ``rclcpp``. Consult each backend's own documentation "
"for its full API surface."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:177
msgid "Python"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:179
msgid ""
"``rclpy`` exposes the same option as a keyword argument on ``Node."
"create_subscription``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:182
msgid ""
"self.create_subscription(\n"
"    Image,\n"
"    'image',\n"
"    self.image_callback,\n"
"    10,\n"
"    acceptable_buffer_backends='cuda')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:191
msgid ""
"On the Python side the ``data`` field still surfaces as a byte sequence; "
"backend user-facing APIs are currently C++-only, so Python subscribers that "
"accept non-CPU backends typically rely on the backend's own CPU-fallback "
"path (the RMW serializes the buffer to CPU if the backend cannot serve the "
"Python endpoint directly)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:198
msgid "Publisher side"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:200
msgid ""
"Publishers use the backend's allocation API to produce a message whose "
"``uint8[]`` field is already backed by that backend's memory. For example, "
"with the CUDA backend:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:204
msgid ""
"#include \"cuda_buffer/cuda_buffer_api.hpp\"\n"
"\n"
"sensor_msgs::msg::Image msg;\n"
"msg.data = cuda_buffer_backend::allocate_buffer(width * height * 3);\n"
"msg.header.stamp = this->now();\n"
"msg.width = width;\n"
"msg.height = height;\n"
"msg.encoding = \"rgb8\";\n"
"msg.step = width * 3;\n"
"\n"
"{\n"
"  auto wh = cuda_buffer_backend::from_output_buffer(msg.data, stream_);\n"
"  my_kernel<<<grid, block, 0, stream_>>>(wh.get_ptr(), ...);\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"publisher_->publish(msg);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:223
msgid ""
"A publisher does not need any ``rclcpp`` option changes; the backend of the "
"buffer inside the message is what the RMW sees at publish time. If a given "
"subscriber has not opted in to that backend, the RMW falls back to CPU "
"serialization for that peer transparently -- the publisher writes the same "
"message either way."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:230
msgid "QoS considerations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:232
msgid ""
"QoS compatibility is still checked by the RMW implementation in the usual "
"way. The backend selection controls how eligible buffer fields are "
"represented once a sample is being delivered; it does not replace DDS "
"reliability, history, deadline, lifespan, or liveliness behavior."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:235
msgid ""
"The CUDA backend's zero-copy path is intended for live topic samples. Use "
"volatile durability for CUDA-backed topics. Transient-local durability for "
"late-joining subscribers is not a supported zero-copy CUDA use case, because "
"the descriptor refers to publisher-owned live memory that may be recycled by "
"the backend."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:240
msgid "Tensor messages with ``torch_conversions``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:242
msgid ""
"The experimental tensor path uses a normal ROS 2 message, ``tensor_msgs/msg/"
"ExperimentalTensor``. Its ``data`` field is a ``rosidl::Buffer<uint8_t>``, "
"while the other fields carry DLPack-aligned tensor metadata such as dtype, "
"shape, strides, and byte offset. The ``torch_conversions`` package is a "
"header-only helper library that fills that message and converts it to or "
"from ``at::Tensor``; it does not register a separate buffer backend. "
"Framework-specific packages can follow the same pattern for other tensor "
"libraries. See :doc:`../Tutorials/Advanced/Writing-a-Buffer-Compatible-"
"Conversions-Package` for guidance on writing a ``*_conversions`` package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:255
msgid "On the publisher side:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:257
msgid ""
"#include \"torch_conversions/torch_conversions.hpp\"\n"
"#include \"tensor_msgs/msg/experimental_tensor.hpp\"\n"
"\n"
"auto guard = torch_conversions::set_stream();\n"
"auto msg = torch_conversions::allocate_tensor_msg(\n"
"  {height, width, 4}, torch::kByte, c10::kCUDA);\n"
"torch_conversions::to_tensor_msg(msg, rendered_frame);\n"
"publisher_->publish(msg);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:268
msgid ""
"On the subscriber side, opt in to the storage backend you want to accept and "
"then convert the received tensor message:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:271
msgid ""
"rclcpp::SubscriptionOptions sub_opts;\n"
"sub_opts.acceptable_buffer_backends = \"cuda\";\n"
"\n"
"subscription_ = this->create_subscription<tensor_msgs::msg::"
"ExperimentalTensor>(\n"
"  \"image\", 10,\n"
"  [](const tensor_msgs::msg::ExperimentalTensor::SharedPtr msg) {\n"
"    auto guard = torch_conversions::set_stream();\n"
"    at::Tensor frame =\n"
"      torch_conversions::from_input_tensor_msg(*msg, /*clone=*/false);\n"
"    consume(frame);\n"
"  },\n"
"  sub_opts);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:287
msgid "Ensuring a compatible pub/sub pair"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:289
msgid ""
"Aside from matching backend names (``\"cuda\"`` on both sides, and so on), "
"there are three practical rules for making a non-CPU path actually work end-"
"to-end."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:294
msgid "1. Install the same backend on both sides"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:296
msgid ""
"Both the backend plugin package **and** its descriptor-message package "
"(``*_backend_msgs``) must be installed and available to the process. If the "
"subscriber is missing either, it will silently fall back to CPU and log a "
"warning from the RMW buffer-backend loader."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:301
msgid "Check with:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:303
msgid "$ ros2 pkg list | grep cuda_buffer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:307
msgid ""
"Libraries built on top of ``rosidl::Buffer`` may have their own "
"dependencies. For example, ``torch_conversions`` uses the ``cuda`` backend "
"for CUDA tensor storage when the CUDA packages are available, but it does "
"not register a separate buffer backend name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:313
msgid "2. Use the same RMW on both sides"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:315
msgid ""
"Use the same RMW implementation for publisher and subscriber. Buffer "
"descriptors are serialized through the RMW's normal type-support pipeline, "
"so the ordinary advice for :doc:`multiple RMW implementations <Working-with-"
"multiple-RMW-implementations>` applies: set ``RMW_IMPLEMENTATION`` "
"consistently."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:322
msgid "3. Keep backend versions aligned with ROS 2 core"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:324
msgid ""
"Backend plugins are ABI-coupled to ``rosidl_buffer_backend`` and to the "
"RMW's type-support library. Rebuild installed backends after upgrading ROS 2 "
"core, the same way you would rebuild any other plugin package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:330
msgid "Transport scope depends on the backend"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:332
msgid ""
"``rosidl::Buffer`` only guarantees that the descriptor round-trips through "
"the RMW. Whether a given ``backend`` can actually carry data intra-process, "
"inter-process on the same host, or inter-host is entirely a property of the "
"backend implementation. Consult each backend's own documentation for its "
"support matrix."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:339
msgid "For reference, the CUDA backend currently supports:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:341
msgid "intra-process (same Python/C++ process);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:342
msgid "inter-process on the same host, same GPU, same user (via CUDA VMM IPC);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:343
msgid ""
"inter-host CUDA transport is not currently supported by this backend, so it "
"declines that peer and the publish/subscribe path uses CPU serialization for "
"the field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:345
msgid ""
"Other backends have their own constraints, documented in their own "
"repositories."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:349
msgid "Diagnostics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:352
msgid "Inspecting negotiated transport"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:354
msgid ""
"Inside a subscription callback, ``msg->data.get_backend_type()`` returns the "
"backend of the just-received message (``\"cpu\"``, ``\"cuda\"``, ...). "
"Comparing it to what you expected is the quickest way to tell whether the "
"zero-copy path actually engaged or the RMW fell back to CPU."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:360
msgid "Listing loaded backends at runtime"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:362
msgid "From C++, the registry exposes a list of discovered plugins:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:364
msgid ""
"#include \"rosidl_buffer_backend_registry/buffer_backend_registry.hpp\"\n"
"\n"
"rosidl_buffer_backend_registry::BufferBackendRegistry registry;\n"
"for (const auto & name : registry.get_backend_names()) {\n"
"  RCLCPP_INFO(rclcpp::get_logger(\"app\"), \"Loaded buffer backend: %s\", "
"name.c_str());\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:373
msgid ""
"Most backends also log a warning when they fall back to CPU serialization, "
"which is visible at the default log level."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:377
msgid "Interaction with other transport features"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:379
msgid ""
":doc:`Loaned messages <Configure-ZeroCopy-loaned-messages>` operate at a "
"different layer: they let the RMW own the *message* memory, while ``rosidl::"
"Buffer`` backends control the storage of individual variable-length array "
"fields. The two features can be combined when both the RMW and the backend "
"support it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/How-To-Guides/Using-Buffer-Backends.rst:385
msgid ""
":doc:`Intra-process communication <../Tutorials/Demos/Intra-Process-"
"Communication>` is orthogonal: a backend may implement its own intra-process "
"fast path (the CUDA backend does), but the decision is up to the backend."
msgstr ""
